#limitation(title: [The Alliance-and-Recovery Evidence Has No Severity Stratification])[
The entire evidence base supporting the therapeutic-alliance and "recovery-conceivable"
reading is anchored in ambulatory, largely mild-to-moderate patients. None of the load-bearing
studies stratify by ME/CFS severity: the alliance cohort (n=217) and the recovery systematic
review do not report a severe or very-severe subgroup @Heins2013TherapeuticRelationship
@Ingman2022RecoveryRCTReview, and Ingman explicitly cautions that its findings may not
generalise to severe ME/CFS. The HCP-perspective study reports no patient severity at all, and
its purposively recruited patients were likely engaged and receptive @Bakken2026RecoveryConceivable.

Whether embodied improvement and epistemic trust operate at all for the bedbound patient — for
whom "relearning to interpret bodily signals" collides with genuine physiological fragility and
post-exertional symptom exacerbation — is unevidenced. The alliance finding is therefore best
read as a claim about the ambulatory majority, not as a recovery mechanism that extends to the
severe and very-severe patients where psychobehavioural framing has historically done the most
harm (@sec:psychogenic-debate).

*Consequence:* The trust-building insight should not be silently generalised to severe ME/CFS,
where a "recovery is conceivable" framing has materially different risks than it does for an
ambulatory patient.

] <lim:alliance-recovery-severity-gap>
